{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f17e39d99b57bc5b13", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Atomic Physics - Test 3", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d9245147"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450fe", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d9245094", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4766"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b2d"], "question": {"text": "Considering the Bohr model of hydrogen like atoms, the ratio of the ratio of the radius $5^{\\text {th }}$ orbit of the electron in $\\mathrm{Li}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{He}^{+}$is"}, "options": [{"id": "c6fe75", "text": "$\\frac{3}{2}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d9245095"}, {"id": "49bdfc", "text": "$\\frac{4}{9}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d9245096"}, {"id": "90222b", "text": "$\\frac{9}{4}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d9245097"}, {"id": "bde210", "text": "$\\frac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d9245098"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d9245099", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3c"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e43"], "question": {"text": "The atomic hydrogen emits a line spectrum consisting of various series. Which series of hydrogen atomic spectra is lying in the visible region?"}, "options": [{"id": "ec2a8d", "text": "Brackett series", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d924509a"}, {"id": "307773", "text": "Paschen series", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d924509b"}, {"id": "e8a348", "text": "Lyman series", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d924509c"}, {"id": "9b00f7", "text": "Balmer series", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d924509d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d924509e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4766"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87d3"], "question": {"text": "According to Bohr's theory, the moment of momentum of an electron revolving in $4^{\\text {th }}$ orbit of hydrogen atom is:"}, "options": [{"id": "f3f860", "text": "$\\frac{h}{\\pi}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d924509f"}, {"id": "34af28", "text": "$\\frac{h}{2 \\pi}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a0"}, {"id": "12952a", "text": "$8 \\frac{h}{\\pi}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a1"}, {"id": "6ae7c5", "text": "$2 \\frac{h}{\\pi}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a2"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a3", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3c"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5de3"], "question": {"text": "During the transition of electron from state A to state C of a Bohr atom, the wavelength of emitted radiation is $2000 Å$ and it becomes $6000 Å$ when the electron jumps from state B to state C. Then the wavelength of the radiation emitted during the transition of electrons from state A to state B is"}, "options": [{"id": "a07a7a", "text": "$4000 Å$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a4"}, {"id": "f2e79e", "text": "$2000 Å$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a5"}, {"id": "849b25", "text": "$3000 Å$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a6"}, {"id": "bba7eb", "text": "$6000 Å$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a7"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a8", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4766"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b471c"], "question": {"text": "In Bohr&#39;s atomic model of hydrogen, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> are the kinetic energy, potential energy and total energy of the electron respectively. Choose the correct option when the electron undergoes transitions to a higher level :"}, "options": [{"id": "202092", "text": "All&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> increase.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450a9"}, {"id": "e65819", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> decreases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> increase.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450aa"}, {"id": "993917", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> decreases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> increase.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450ab"}, {"id": "c7bddf", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> increases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> decrease", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450ac"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450ad", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3c"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e43"], "question": {"text": "The longest wavelength associated with Paschen series is : (Given $\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{H}}=1.097 \\times 10^7 \\mathrm{SI}$ unit)"}, "options": [{"id": "4fd97d", "text": "$3.646 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~m}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450ae"}, {"id": "22922d", "text": "$1.876 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~m}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450af"}, {"id": "5759db", "text": "$2.973 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~m}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b0"}, {"id": "21fa85", "text": "$1.094 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~m}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b1"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b2", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4766"], "concepts": ["6162fb5b6ed13040ec84df6b"], "question": {"text": "The electron in a hydrogen atom first jumps from the third excited state to the second excited state and subsequently to the first excited state. The ratio of the respective wavelengths&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math> of the photons emitted in this process is:"}, "options": [{"id": "c5d9eb", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b3"}, {"id": "148786", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b4"}, {"id": "2ba280", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b5"}, {"id": "d1fba9", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>27</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b7", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3c"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5de3"], "question": {"text": "If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are the wavelengths of the third member of Lyman and first member of the Paschen series respectively, then the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is :"}, "options": [{"id": "e97159", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>135</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b8"}, {"id": "b60ed7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>9</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450b9"}, {"id": "915b9d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450ba"}, {"id": "7632c0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>108</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450bb"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450bc", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4766"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b471c"], "question": {"text": "The mass of proton, neutron and helium nucleus are respectively <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0073</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0087</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0015</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi></math>. The binding energy of helium nucleus is:"}, "options": [{"id": "2040dd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>14</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>MeV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450bd"}, {"id": "e977e3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>28</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>MeV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450be"}, {"id": "022383", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>56</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>MeV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450bf"}, {"id": "2c8a11", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>MeV</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c1", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3c"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e43"], "question": {"text": "A hydrogen atom in ground state is given an energy of $10.2 \\mathrm{eV}$. How many spectral lines will be emitted due to transition of electrons?"}, "options": [{"id": "16f786", "text": "6", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c2"}, {"id": "a16596", "text": "3", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c3"}, {"id": "426b76", "text": "10", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c4"}, {"id": "48e5fc", "text": "1", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c6", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4766"], "concepts": ["6162fb5b6ed13040ec84df6b"], "question": {"text": "In a Frank - Hertz experiment, an electron of energy <math><mn>5.6</mn><mtext>&#8201;</mtext><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></math>&nbsp;passes through mercury vapour and emerges with an energy <math><mn>0.7</mn><mtext>&#8201;</mtext><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The minimum wavelength of photons emitted by mercury atoms is close to:"}, "options": [{"id": "678808", "text": "<math><mn>250</mn><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c7"}, {"id": "29552e", "text": "<math><mn>1700</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c8"}, {"id": "bde1d6", "text": "<math><mn>220</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450c9"}, {"id": "da667e", "text": "<math><mn>2020</mn><mi> </mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450ca"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450cb", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3c"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5de3"], "question": {"text": "<p>In the given figure, the energy levels of hydrogen atom have been shown along with some transitions marked <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math>.<br />The transitions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> respectively represent</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fb5eee439-3dc7-4999-be99-a86093c89280-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 253px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "f775d0", "text": "The ionization potential of hydrogen, second member of Balmer series and third member of Paschen series.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450cc"}, {"id": "bb47e2", "text": "The series limit of Lyman series, second member of Balmer series and second member of Paschen series.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450cd"}, {"id": "eaa46f", "text": "The series limit of Lyman series, third member of Balmer series and second member of Paschen series.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450ce"}, {"id": "d25c4f", "text": "The first member of the Lyman series, third member of Balmer series and second member of Paschen series.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450cf"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450d0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60d2d1d2185d7a32fa3205ac"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4766"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87d3"], "question": {"text": "If an electron is moving in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mtext>th&#160;</mtext></msup></math> orbit of the hydrogen atom, then its velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mstyle mathvariant=\"italic\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mstyle></math> for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mtext>th&#160;</mtext></msup></math> orbit is given as:"}, "options": [{"id": "1d8e21", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#8733;</mo><mi>n</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f272ee0e063d92450d1"}, {"id": "bd4f98", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://